codex
Implemented and locally verified a native Track B generator based on the paper’s cycle-closure lattice map and the co-tree minor identity in Proposition 2.24’s proof. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1303.6478)

Deliverables:

- [gen_1303_6478.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1303.6478/gen_1303_6478.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1303.6478/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1303.6478/README.md)
- [bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1303.6478/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1303.6478/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1303.6478/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Local results:

- All G1–G9(c) gates pass.
- Structure-aware guessing: 0/200,000.
- Seven attacks: each 0/8.
- Reference Bareiss algorithm: 1,161,484 exact operations, about 0.095 seconds.
- Compact co-tree route: 128 operations.
- Worst-case answer: 34 characters, approximately 9 tokens, 12 elements.
- Deterministic generation, JSON round-trip, doubled-size verification, and canonical-key invariance all pass.

The required four-vendor oracle stage remains blocked: the configured OpenRouter key reports `limit_remaining: 0`, and every script-owned call returned HTTP 403. These are correctly recorded as 0 scored attempts—not as model failures—so the result is not falsely labeled hardened or ready to submit. Replenishing or replacing `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` is the remaining external step.
