     1	# Tropical cycle-closure determinant generator (arXiv:1303.6478)
     2	
     3	> Build status: the generator and every local gate pass, but this result is **not
     4	> ready to submit or call hardened**.  The required bare, structural-hint, and
     5	> placebo oracle runs all aborted because the configured OpenRouter key reports
     6	> `limit_remaining: 0`.  Their script-owned error transcripts are retained; no
     7	> API error is counted as a model failure.
     8	
     9	## Profile
    10	
    11	| field | value |
    12	|---|---|
    13	| track | **B — no-tool compression** |
    14	| native domain | algebra |
    15	| object regime | integer lattice |
    16	| computational core | linear algebra |
    17	| certificate form | exact symbolic prime-exponent vector |
    18	| intuition | invariant: a co-tree minor is an edge-weight product despite basis scrambling |
    19	| domain essentiality | native |
    20	| reduction | none |
    21	
    22	## The problem and why the local result is trustworthy
    23	
    24	This family comes from Buchholz and Markwig, [*Tropical covers of curves and
    25	their moduli spaces*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.6478).  A solver receives a
    26	weighted trivalent tropical cover of the tripod-shaped tropical line, a
    27	spanning tree, and a dense integral matrix representing the associated
    28	cycle-closure minor after integral basis changes.  It must return the prime
    29	exponents of the absolute determinant.
    30	
    31	The generator makes a chain of balanced diamonds.  Every finite source length
    32	is the exact rational target distance divided by its edge weight.  The center
    33	has genus 29 and profile `u=(60), v=(1,59), w=(60)`; every other vertex is
    34	trivalent, genus zero, and has RH-number one.  Thus the object is a genuine
    35	cover under Definition 2.2, not a graph analogue.  `verify` independently
    36	checks connectivity, genus, end profiles, slopes, balancing, RH labels, the
    37	spanning-tree complement, the dense determinant by Bareiss elimination, and
    38	the proposed prime factorization.  It never reads `inst["answer"]`.
    39	
    40	Generation is by composition of identities.  In the fundamental-cycle basis,
    41	the selected minor is diagonal with the co-tree edge weights.  Unit triangular
    42	integer matrices change its two lattice bases and preserve the absolute
    43	determinant.  The answer is therefore tallied before the dense matrix is made;
    44	the generator never solves its emitted matrix.  Definitions 2.16 and 2.19
    45	define the cycle equations and their lattice index.  The graph-theoretic
    46	argument in the proof of Proposition 2.24 gives the spanning-tree minor
    47	identity; although the proposition's cone-weight comparison is stated for
    48	rational covers, that part of its proof uses only the connected graph and its
    49	cycle basis.  Lemma 3.2 places the same lattice factors in the branch-map
    50	multiplicity.  The profile says `algebra`, not `geometry`, because the object
    51	the solver ultimately computes with is this native integer lattice map.
    52	
    53	## Track B hardness
    54	
    55	The paper does not prove a complexity-theoretic hardness result, so Track A
    56	would be unjustified.  The disclosed general route is fraction-free Bareiss
    57	elimination, `O(n^3)` exact integer arithmetic, followed by division over the
    58	fixed prime basis.  At shipping `n=96`, the eight-seed reference panel solved
    59	8/8 and averaged **1,161,484 exact operations and 0.095 seconds** in the final
    60	recorded selftest.  This is easy with a program and not executable by hand from
    61	a dense 96×96 matrix.
    62	
    63	The compact route uses the minor argument in the proof of Proposition 2.24:
    64	integral basis changes do not alter the absolute determinant, while the
    65	fundamental-cycle/co-tree matrix has one edge weight per pivot.  Tallying the
    66	96 named co-tree primes takes at most 128 small exact operations.  Section
    67	3.1's one-dimensional star resolutions were
    68	avoided as an easy regime: there a transposition merely cuts or joins cycles.
    69	Likewise, once a full combinatorial type and branch images are supplied, its
    70	metric lengths are direct distance/weight evaluations; that is why the prior
    71	“recover lengths” triage was not used.
    72	
    73	## Worked demo (complete rendered instance)
    74	
    75	This is `make_instance(n=4, mix_bound=1, seed=2)`.  A person can solve it on
    76	paper: ignore the scrambled determinant, take the listed co-tree weights
    77	`53,17,29,17`, and tally their prime exponents.
    78	
    79	```text
    80	Tropical cycle-closure determinant
    81	
    82	The target tropical line is a tripod with center c and rays u, v, w.  A source
    83	vertex mapped to c has the three ray directions; a source vertex at a positive
    84	coordinate on u has a left and a right direction.  Every finite source edge is
    85	mapped linearly to its listed ray with positive integer slope called its weight.
    86	Its target distance equals weight times source length.  At each source vertex,
    87	the sums of adjacent weights in every target direction must agree; their common
    88	value is the local degree.  The Riemann-Hurwitz number is
    89	
    90	  r(V) = valence(V) + 2*genus(V) - 2
    91	         - local_degree(V)*(target_valence-2).
    92	
    93	It must be nonnegative and equal the number of labels at V.  Ends have infinite
    94	length.  These rules define the tropical cover below; all integers and rational
    95	lengths are exact.  Vertex and edge identifiers are zero-based labels only.
    96	
    97	Degree: 60
    98	Source genus: 33
    99	Ramification profile: u=[60], v=[1, 59], w=[60]
   100	
   101	VERTICES
   102	V0 target=c pos=0/1 genus=29 labels=-
   103	V1 target=u pos=1/1 genus=0 labels=1
   104	V2 target=u pos=2/1 genus=0 labels=2
   105	V3 target=u pos=3/1 genus=0 labels=3
   106	V4 target=u pos=4/1 genus=0 labels=4
   107	V5 target=u pos=5/1 genus=0 labels=5
   108	V6 target=u pos=6/1 genus=0 labels=6
   109	V7 target=u pos=7/1 genus=0 labels=7
   110	V8 target=u pos=8/1 genus=0 labels=8
   111	
   112	EDGES
   113	E0 V0 END ray=v weight=1 length=infinity
   114	E1 V0 END ray=v weight=59 length=infinity
   115	E2 V0 END ray=w weight=60 length=infinity
   116	E3 V0 V1 ray=u weight=60 length=1/60
   117	E4 V1 V2 ray=u weight=53 length=1/53
   118	E5 V1 V2 ray=u weight=7 length=1/7
   119	E6 V2 V3 ray=u weight=60 length=1/60
   120	E7 V3 V4 ray=u weight=43 length=1/43
   121	E8 V3 V4 ray=u weight=17 length=1/17
   122	E9 V4 V5 ray=u weight=60 length=1/60
   123	E10 V5 V6 ray=u weight=29 length=1/29
