   245	```
   246	
   247	## Caveats
   248	
   249	- Recognizing the co-tree argument in the proof of Proposition 2.24 makes the
   250	  task intentionally easy: it becomes a 96-item frequency tally.  This is
   251	  Track B, not a structural-hardness claim.
   252	- The 0/200,000 guess result is uniform over weak compositions satisfying every
   253	  stated shape and sum constraint.  It says nothing about a solver that uses
   254	  the co-tree invariant or statistical priors over the generator's prime pairs.
   255	- The panel tried equal counts, smaller-branch selection, diagonal valuations,
   256	  row and column contents, first-nonzero-column factors, and 4,096 random
   257	  restarts per seed.  It did not test a CAS Smith normal form, modular
   258	  determinant/CRT, or LLL; Bareiss is the successful exact reference instead.
   259	- The proof of Proposition 2.24 explicitly changes the cycle basis.  This
   260	  generator also expresses the restricted co-tree lattice in a changed integral
   261	  domain basis; determinant invariance under that second basis change is standard
   262	  lattice linear algebra, not a separately numbered result in the paper.
   263	- The benchmark isolates the lattice-minor ingredient of the paper, not the
   264	  full enumeration of branch-map fibers or tropical Hurwitz numbers.
   265	- Most importantly, the four-vendor hardness claim and all G9 arm conclusions
   266	  remain unverified until the OpenRouter key limit is restored.

